(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["fetal"]="<h1>Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-fetus-need-shortcuts\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why does the fetus need shortcuts?</h2>\n<p>Before birth, the <strong>placenta</strong>, not the lungs, does gas exchange. The fetal lungs are filled with fluid, and their vessels are tightly constricted, so <strong>pulmonary resistance is very high</strong>. Pumping all the blood through them would be wasted effort.</p>\n<p>So the fetal circulation has <strong>three shortcuts (shunts)</strong> that send oxygen-rich blood where it is needed, mainly the heart and brain, and route most of it <strong>past the liver and lungs</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Anatomical illustration tracing placenta, umbilical veins and arteries, fetal heart, ductus venosus, foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus\"><figcaption><strong>Complete fetal circulation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-trace-the-fetal-circuit\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Trace the fetal circuit</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"placenta\">Placenta</span> → umbilical vein:</strong> the most oxygen-rich blood in the fetus (yet still only about 80% saturated) travels up the <strong>single umbilical vein</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 1: ductus venosus.</strong> Much of it <strong>bypasses the liver</strong>, going straight to the inferior vena cava.</li>\n<li><strong>Into the right atrium.</strong> The stream from the IVC is aimed straight across the atrium at an opening in the septum.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 2: foramen ovale.</strong> This oxygen-rich stream crosses <strong>from the right atrium to the left atrium</strong>, then goes to the left ventricle → aorta → <strong>heart and brain first</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SVC blood</strong> (oxygen-poor, from the head) goes mainly into the right ventricle → pulmonary trunk.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 3: ductus arteriosus.</strong> Pulmonary resistance is high, so most of this blood <strong>bypasses the lungs</strong>, crossing <strong>from the pulmonary trunk into the descending aorta</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Back to the placenta:</strong> the <strong>two umbilical arteries</strong> (branches of the internal iliac arteries) carry blood back to be reoxygenated.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Shunt</th>\n  <th>Connects</th>\n  <th>Bypasses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n  <td>Umbilical vein → inferior vena cava</td>\n  <td>The liver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></td>\n  <td>Right atrium → left atrium</td>\n  <td>The lungs (via the right ventricle)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n  <td>Pulmonary trunk → aorta</td>\n  <td>The lungs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does blood flow right-to-left through the shunts?</strong> Because, before birth, the <strong>right side</strong> has the higher pressure: pulmonary resistance is high, while the placenta keeps systemic resistance low.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-fetal-haemoglobin-grabbing-oxygen-from-the-mother\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Fetal haemoglobin: grabbing oxygen from the mother</h2>\n<p><strong>Fetal haemoglobin (HbF)</strong> holds oxygen <strong>more tightly</strong> than adult haemoglobin (HbA), because it binds the molecule <strong>2,3-BPG</strong> less strongly. 2,3-BPG normally loosens haemoglobin's grip on oxygen. So, at the low oxygen levels in the placenta, fetal blood can <strong>pull oxygen across from the mother's blood</strong>. After birth, HbF is gradually replaced by HbA over several months.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why is most blood diverted away from the fetal lungs?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The fetal lungs are fluid-filled and have very high vascular resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The fetal lungs already receive oxygen from amniotic fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The fetal heart is too weak to pump blood through the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The lungs would take oxygen away from the placenta</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"fetal:e3906c2c\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Gas exchange happens in the placenta. The fluid-filled lungs' constricted vessels resist flow, so blood takes the shunts.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Which shunt carries blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ductus venosus</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The foramen ovale</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ductus arteriosus</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The umbilical artery</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"fetal:6dbfe2c5\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The ductus arteriosus links the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta, bypassing the lungs.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does fetal haemoglobin help the fetus take oxygen from the mother?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It binds 2,3-BPG more strongly, so it releases oxygen easily</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It holds oxygen more tightly because it binds 2,3-BPG less strongly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It carries carbon dioxide instead of oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It is found only in the placenta, not in fetal blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"fetal:ae4ac64b\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> With less 2,3-BPG bound, HbF has a higher affinity for oxygen, so it loads oxygen from maternal blood.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-closes-after-delivery\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> What closes after delivery?</h2>\n<p>Two events at birth flip the pressures:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The first breaths:</strong> the lungs fill with air and oxygen rises, so the lung vessels <strong>dilate</strong> and <strong>pulmonary resistance falls sharply</strong>. Lung blood flow surges, and much more blood returns to the <strong>left atrium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Clamping the cord:</strong> the placenta, a <strong>low-resistance</strong> circuit, is cut off, so <strong>systemic resistance rises</strong>. The flow up the umbilical vein stops.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure dg-figure\"><figcaption>At birth — two changes that redirect circulation</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 318\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two chains. First breaths: lung vessels dilate and pulmonary resistance falls, more blood returns to the left atrium, left atrial pressure exceeds right, so the foramen ovale shuts. Cord clamped: the low-resistance placenta is removed so systemic resistance rises, umbilical venous flow stops and the ductus venosus closes.\"><defs><marker id=\"fb-m\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head\"/></marker><marker id=\"fb-b\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-blue\"/></marker><marker id=\"fb-r\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-red\"/></marker><marker id=\"fb-g\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-gold\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"6\" y=\"10\" width=\"168\" height=\"39\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"34\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">First breaths</text><path d=\"M90 51V69\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#fb-m)\"/><rect x=\"6\" y=\"73\" width=\"168\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue-soft\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"97\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lung vessels dilate:</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"116\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">resistance falls</text><path d=\"M90 133V151\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#fb-m)\"/><rect x=\"6\" y=\"155\" width=\"168\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue-soft\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"179\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">More blood returns</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"198\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">to the left atrium</text><path d=\"M90 215V233\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#fb-m)\"/><rect x=\"6\" y=\"237\" width=\"168\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue-soft\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"261\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">LA pressure > RA:</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"280\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">foramen ovale shuts</text><rect x=\"186\" y=\"10\" width=\"168\" height=\"39\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-green\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"34\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cord clamped</text><path d=\"M270 51V69\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#fb-m)\"/><rect x=\"186\" y=\"73\" width=\"168\" height=\"77\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-green-soft\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"97\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Placenta removed:</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"116\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">systemic resistance</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"135\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">rises</text><path d=\"M270 152V170\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#fb-m)\"/><rect x=\"186\" y=\"174\" width=\"168\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-green-soft\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"198\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Umbilical vein</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"217\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">flow stops</text><path d=\"M270 234V252\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#fb-m)\"/><rect x=\"186\" y=\"256\" width=\"168\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-green-soft\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"280\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ductus venosus</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"299\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">closes</text></svg><p>These simultaneous changes shift atrial pressures, favor functional foramen ovale closure and contribute to ductus arteriosus constriction. Anatomical closure occurs later and varies by structure.</p></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Medical illustration of neonatal heart circulation showing postnatal pulmonary blood flow and altered fetal shunt pathways\"><figcaption><strong>Circulation after birth.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Now <strong>the left side has the higher pressure</strong>, and each shunt closes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Shunt</th>\n  <th>Why it closes</th>\n  <th>Becomes (anatomical remnant)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></td>\n  <td>Left atrial pressure now <strong>exceeds</strong> right, which presses the flap shut</td>\n  <td><strong>Fossa ovalis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Higher oxygen</strong> and <strong>less prostaglandin</strong> (from the placenta) make it constrict</td>\n  <td><strong>Ligamentum arteriosum</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n  <td>No more umbilical venous flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Ligamentum venosum</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Umbilical vein</td>\n  <td>No more flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Ligamentum teres</strong> (of the liver)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Umbilical arteries</td>\n  <td>No more flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Medial umbilical ligaments</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Closure happens in two steps:</strong> <strong>functional</strong> closure within minutes to days, as flow stops; then <strong>anatomical</strong> closure over weeks to months, as tissue seals the channel.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Section 4\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Section 4 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does the foramen ovale close at birth?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Prostaglandin levels rise after the cord is clamped</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Left atrial pressure becomes higher than right atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The umbilical vein carries more blood after birth</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pulmonary resistance rises when the lungs fill with air</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"fetal:5130a9e9\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> More blood returns from the lungs to the left atrium, and its pressure rises above the right, pressing the flap shut.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What makes the ductus arteriosus constrict after birth?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Falling oxygen and rising prostaglandins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A rise in pulmonary vascular resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Increased flow through the umbilical arteries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"fetal:8d978920\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Oxygen constricts the ductus, and the prostaglandins that kept it open fall once the placenta is gone.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> At birth, pulmonary resistance falls while systemic resistance rises. Why do both happen?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both are caused by the first breath alone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Breathing dilates the lung vessels; cord clamping removes the low-resistance placenta</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Both are caused by clamping the cord alone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The ductus arteriosus closing raises both resistances</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"fetal:2a018911\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> They are two separate events: lung aeration lowers pulmonary resistance, and losing the placenta raises systemic resistance.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"5-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The placenta</strong> does fetal gas exchange. The fluid-filled lungs have high resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Three shunts:</strong> ductus venosus (bypasses the liver), foramen ovale (right → left atrium), ductus arteriosus (pulmonary trunk → aorta).</li>\n<li><strong>The best-oxygenated blood</strong> goes through the foramen ovale to the heart and brain.</li>\n<li><strong>Before birth the right side has the higher pressure</strong>, so flow through the shunts is right-to-left.</li>\n<li><strong>HbF</strong> binds oxygen more tightly (less 2,3-BPG binding), so it loads oxygen from the mother.</li>\n<li><strong>At birth:</strong> the first breaths lower pulmonary resistance; cord clamping raises systemic resistance; <strong>left pressure &gt; right</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Remnants:</strong> fossa ovalis, ligamentum arteriosum, ligamentum venosum, ligamentum teres, medial umbilical ligaments.</li>\n</ol>\n";
